{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And Moses cried out to YHWH.</b> He was distressed over his sister and prayed on her behalf. And the sense of <b>saying</b> is that the prayer was in explicit speech, not in thought alone, and this is what the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, said: one who prays must articulate with his lips. And the reason for articulating is so that the letters are formed by the breath of a man's mouth in voice and breath and speech, and the prayer, thus formed in breath, ascends before Him, may He be exalted." "<b>El, please, heal her now.</b> El — whose power is in Your hand; I beseech You, heal her now. And this Name has judgment included within mercy. But in most places we find it as the attribute of kindness, as (Psalms 22:2): ״אלי אלי למה עזבתני״, and likewise (Psalms 50): ״אל אלהים ה' דבר ויקרא ארץ״, which is the order of the attributes — kindness, judgment, mercy — through which the world was created; and so it is written (Psalms 52): ״חסד אל כל היום״, and it is written (Psalms 99): ״אל נושא היית להם״. And what is written (Psalms 7): ״ואל זועם בכל יום״ — its meaning is 'in every day,' that the attribute of judgment is found, yet there is a day when it is not found, for the attribute of kindness displaces it, in the manner mentioned in connection with Abraham."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:57.039887417Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}